{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789785816,"nonce":"1R2KtVgWsW56mJXje73V4UHtU+I=","message":"Done in c7bfe72, as a test in front of the existing poll rather than a loop split. NUC pinned to 4 cores, 9-11 iterations vs 4281cea: closure_call -25% (Cranelift) / -16% (full-jit), method_call full-jit -10%, tiered_hotloop -8 to -13%, mandelbrot_small -2 to -6%, fib hybrid -11%, nbody -1%; free/inlined_call and hybrid-auto method_call move +1 to +4%, at the level of a tenth of a cycle per iteration. Three loop-splitting shapes were measured and rejected before this: a strip loop around the inner one (with and without rotating the inner loop's exit test to its latch) and a poll block on the latch path. All of them route the loop-carried values through a block with two param-carrying successors, and both regalloc2 and LLVM answered with a spill and a reload of the accumulator on every iteration -- Cranelift method_call +25%, free_call +5%, disassembly confirmed -- which cost more than the poll. Loops stepping by a variable amount keep the per-iteration poll: their low bits are not bounded. Strip-mining proper, with the poll call out of the loop, would need a shape that keeps the latch's parameter traffic unchanged; the counted form of the earlier attempt is in the history of this issue if that is tried again.","files":null}]}